(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608123600")
  (:adr/title "kotoba-server's operator bearer is a secret the system publishes")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-12")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608122600" "ADR-2608122800" "ADR-2607072000"))
  (:adr/body
  "## The question that was asked, and its answer

ADR-2608122600 found the `cloud-itonami/actor-*` repos to be the best-defended
code in the fleet, but could not close one caveat: the bridges attach an
`alg:none` unsigned JWT asserting a `sub` DID, and the allowlist contains a LAN
address, not only loopback. **Could anything on that LAN assert an arbitrary
DID?**

**No. That caveat is refuted.** `sub` is compared for string equality against
one configured value and everything else is 401. This is NOT the shape of
ADR-2608122800, where the pod could not distinguish a verified DID from an
asserted one. Here it can, by rejecting every DID but one.

**What is real is one level down.**

## The operator credential is unsigned, never-expiring, and documented to publish

`require_operator_auth` does five things: an internal-trust gate, strip
`Bearer `, check expiry, read `sub`, compare to the operator DID. It **does not
verify the signature and does not look at `alg`** -- `jwt_sub` is
`token.split('.').nth(1)` base64-decoded. Its own doc comment says so: *the
signature is NOT verified -- the edge BFF is the trust boundary*.

Two properties compound:

- **`jwt_exp_elapsed` returns false when there is no `exp` claim.** The actor
  payload is `{\"sub\": …}` only, so the token never expires -- **and neither
  would a forged one.**
- **The only defence against a forged `sub` is `require_internal_trust`**, whose
  own doc comment names this exact threat (*a request that reaches the pod
  directly can forge any `sub`*). Its body reads the secret and, when unset,
  **returns Ok**. That is structurally the `if SECRET and …` shape of
  ADR-2608122800, **in the gate written specifically to stop this**, and
  `KOTOBA_INTERNAL_SECRET` appears nowhere in the fleet's deployment template or
  provisioning plan. It exists only on the cloud gateway path.

And the DID itself is treated as public by design: an actor config comments it
`PUBLIC did:key (never a secret)`, the server logs it at every boot, and one
bridge's docstring tells you to read it from *the node's agent-identity log
line*. **Two full node operator DIDs are committed in this workspace today**,
one of them in a document that says it was copied out of a plaintext log.

`fleet.edn` compounds it once more: the operator seed is described as a **shared
operator DID across the fleet** -- one credential authenticates to every node.

## What holding it obtains

24 operator-authenticated endpoints. The consequential ones:

- **`datomic_transact`, with no graph-ownership check in this branch.** The
  owner-binding block exists only inside the CACAO path. So the caller writes to
  **any graph CID on the node**, is **exempt from the tier write quota** and
  **exempt from the write fee**, is stamped as author, and the commit is signed
  with the node's IPNS key -- **forged writes publish under the node's identity.**
- `datomic_gc` (deletion), `block_put` / `vault_put` / `vault_get`, the whole
  invoke/agent/infer/embed family, `node_register`, `did_document_publish`,
  `didcomm_send`, `atproto_repo_write`, and `econ_credit` (mint to any DID).

**Reads are not obtained**, and that is a real defence rather than an accident:
private graphs take a CACAO-only path with genuine signature verification, owner
binding and nonce replay protection. The firehose gates on the same default.

Also found while tracing: **`/mesh/http/*route` has no authentication of any
kind** and invokes the registered component with the caller's raw body, passing
the operator DID as the principal.

## Reachability

The listener binds **all interfaces**, not loopback and not tailnet-only. The
fleet default port is shared across 12 configured nodes on the same `/24` as the
allowlisted address, five with the binary installed as always-on daemons -- so
roughly six LAN-reachable listeners, not one. **No firewall, bind restriction or
tailnet ACL is expressed anywhere in the workspace**; Tailscale is how the
operator reaches nodes, not a restriction on who else can. Actual host firewall
state is machine-local and was not probed.

## Severity: moderate and conditional, and the actor repos are exonerated

The literal caveat is refuted. What remains is that a **total** credential is an
unverifiable published string, its dedicated defence is unset by the same
short-circuit this workspace spent the day fixing elsewhere, and it is shared
fleet-wide by design.

**The actor bridges did not create this and their conduct is correct.** The
allowlist is checked before I/O at three sites, live traffic needs an explicit
flag, and the header is attached **only when an operator DID is configured** --
so an unconfigured bridge sends no credential and takes a 401, **the exact
opposite of the VPN client's silent omission.** One bridge carries a
member-signed CACAO path that sends no bearer at all. They revealed this; they
are not its cause.

## The finding worth escalating is ownership, not `sub`

**The receiver exists in no current tree.** `kotoba-lang/kotoba` has no
`crates/` directory at checkout, at its west pin, or on `origin/main` -- all
three confirmed. The code was deleted 2026-07-01 (ADR-2607072000, which names
`kotoba-server` as the one crate with no cljc successor, still deployed). The
deployed artifact is pinned in a build manifest to a commit recoverable only
from history, and is *distributed out of band and not in this checkout*.

So: **a binary with 24 operator-authenticated endpoints and one unauthenticated
execution route is resident on five machines, and its source is in no checkout,
no west pin and no default branch.** That is ADR-2608122600's own theme --
main, the pin and the checkout are different things -- arriving at the server
side, with a fourth term: the artifact.

## What could not be determined

- **Whether the deployed binary matches the pinned commit.** Source was read at
  that commit and cross-checked against the last pre-deletion commit; **the
  artifact itself was not.**
- **Whether anything runs on the allowlisted LAN address today**, and whether
  `KOTOBA_INTERNAL_SECRET` is set there -- its launchd plist is machine-local and
  uncommitted. For the fleet the answer is known from the committed template:
  it is not set.
- Host firewall state on any node (no configuration in the repo; not probed).
- Whether the fleet's shared operator DID equals either committed DID -- the
  seed is reported absent from environment, credential store and Keychain, so it
  was not derived and no attempt was made.
- Whether the P2P plane leaks the operator DID: quads carrying it replicate
  across the lattice and there is a second LAN listener. Mesh admission control
  was not traced.
- Whether the `block_put` -> `datomic_transact` -> `generic_invoke` chain
  completes into arbitrary execution. CID resolution was read; the WASM executor
  was not.

**Trees read, per claim.** Server source: repo history at the pinned build
commit, cross-checked against the last commit before deletion. Absence of the
crate: checkout, west pin and `origin/main`, all three. Fleet config: the
murakumo checkout. Actor bridges: **checkout == west pin == GitHub default tip
for all three**, verified by API, so there is no three-way skew on the client
side.")))
